  "text": "Let's break down the problem step by step.\n\n### Step 1: Interpret the given equations\n\n1. The first equation is \\( |25 + 20i - z| = 5 \\). This represents a circle in the complex plane with center \\( 25 + 20i \\) and radius \\( 5 \\).\n\n2. The second equation is \\( |z - 4 - k| = |z - 3i - k| \\). This represents the set of points equidistant from \\( 4 + k \\) and \\( 3i + k \\).\n\n### Step 2: Find the geometric interpretation of the second equation\n\nThe equation \\( |z - 4 - k| = |z - 3i - k| \\) represents the set of points equidistant from \\( 4 + k \\) and \\( 3i + k \\). In the complex plane, these points form a line segment perpendicular to the line joining \\( 4 + k \\) and \\( 3i + k \\) and passing through the midpoint of the segment.\n\nThe midpoint \\( M \\) of the segment joining \\( 4 + k \\) and \\( 3i + k \\) is:\n\\[ M = \\left( \\frac{4 + k}{2}, \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\]\n\nThe line segment is perpendicular to the line joining \\( 4 + k \\) and \\( 3i + k \\), which has a slope of \\( \\frac{k - 3i}{4 + k} \\). The slope of the perpendicular line is the negative reciprocal, which is \\( -\\frac{4 + k}{k - 3i} \\).\n\n### Step 3: Determine the possible values of \\( k \\)\n\nThe first equation represents a circle with center \\( 25 + 20i \\) and radius \\( 5 \\). For the system to have exactly one solution, the circle must be tangent to the line segment. The distance from the center of the circle to the line segment must be equal to the radius of the circle.\n\nThe distance from the center \\( 25 + 20i \\) to the midpoint \\( M = \\left( \\frac{4 + k}{2}, \\frac{k}{2} \\right) \\) is:\n\\[ d = \\sqrt{\\left( 25 - \\frac{4 + k}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 20 - \\frac{k}{2} \\right)^2} \\]\n\nFor the circle to be tangent to the line segment, this distance must be equal to the radius of the circle, which is \\( 5 \\):\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\left( 25 - \\frac{4 + k}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 20 - \\frac{k}{2} \\right)^2} = 5 \\]\n\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\\[ \\left( 25 - \\frac{4 + k}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( 20 - \\frac{k}{2} \\right)^2 = 25 \\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying, we get:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{50 - 4 - k}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{40 - k}{2} \\right)^2 = 25 \\]\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{46 - k}{2} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{40 - k}{2} \\right)^2 = 25 \\]\n\\[ \\frac{(46 - k)^2 + (40 - k)^2}{4} = 25 \\]\n\\[ (46 - k)^2 + (40 - k)^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ 2116 - 92k + k^2 + 1600 - 80k + k^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ 2k^2 - 172k + 3716 = 100 \\]\n\\[ 2k^2 - 172k + 3616 = 0 \\]\n\\[ k^2 - 86k + 1808 = 0 \\]\n\nSolving this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\( k = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\):\n\\[ k = \\frac{86 \\pm \\sqrt{86^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1808}}{2 \\cdot 1} \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{86 \\pm \\sqrt{7396 - 7232}}{2} \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{86 \\pm \\sqrt{164}}{2} \\]\n\\[ k = \\frac{86 \\pm 2\\sqrt{41}}{2} \\]\n\\[ k = 43 \\pm \\sqrt{41} \\]\n\nThe sum of all possible values of \\( k \\) is:\n\\[ (43 + \\sqrt{41}) + (43 - \\sqrt{41}) = 86 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( k \\) is \\( 86 \\), and \\( m+n = 86+1 = 87 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{87}\n\\]",
